# 🧪 TestPilot

**An agent that writes, runs and repairs pytest suites for your Python code, powered by NVIDIA Nemotron on Nebius Token Factory.**

Track: **Coding and Agentic Engineering** · Nebius x NVIDIA Global AI Hackathon 2026

Writing tests is the chore everyone skips. TestPilot reads a Python module, drafts a full pytest suite from the code's names, docstrings and type hints, runs it in a sandbox, and then reasons about every failure: is the *test* wrong, or is your *code* wrong? It fixes bad tests, keeps tests that expose real bugs (marked `xfail` with the reason), and reports coverage at the end.

## How it works

```
your_module.py
      │
      ▼
 Nemotron 3 Nano ──► draft pytest suite
      │
      ▼
 sandbox run (pytest + coverage) ──► all green? ──► done ✅
      │ failures
      ▼
 Nemotron triage: test_bug or code_bug?
      │
      ├─ test_bug ─► rewrite the test
      └─ code_bug ─► keep it, mark xfail, report the bug 🐞
      │
      ▼
 last repair round escalates to Nemotron 3 Super
```

## Where Nemotron and Token Factory are used

| Step | Model (via Token Factory) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Draft tests | `nvidia/NVIDIA-Nemotron-3-Nano-30B-A3B` | Fast and cheap for bulk code generation |
| Triage failures | Nano, then Super on the final round | Distinguishes broken tests from real bugs |
| Repair tests | Nano, escalating to `nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b` | Stronger reasoning only when the cheap model is stuck, so credits stretch further |

All calls go through Token Factory's OpenAI-compatible endpoint (`https://api.tokenfactory.nebius.com/v1/`). Model IDs can be swapped with env vars (`TESTPILOT_FAST_MODEL`, `TESTPILOT_STRONG_MODEL`), e.g. to try `nvidia/Nemotron-3-Ultra-550b-a55b`.

## Setup

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Chetan0246/testpilot.git
cd testpilot
pip install -r requirements.txt
export NEBIUS_API_KEY=your-key   # from tokenfactory.nebius.com → API keys
# or, backup: export NVIDIA_API_KEY=nvapi-...   # from build.nvidia.com (same Nemotron models)
```

## Run it

CLI:
```bash
python -m testpilot examples/inventory.py
```

Web demo:
```bash
streamlit run app.py
```

The `examples/inventory.py` module has three planted bugs (accepts zero quantity, leaves empty items behind, returns items unsorted). TestPilot should flag them.

Example output (illustrative, replace with your own run):
```
Drafting tests for inventory.py with nvidia/NVIDIA-Nemotron-3-Nano-30B-A3B ...
Round 1: 9 passed, 3 failed, 0 errors, coverage 96%
  Bug suspected in your code -> test_items_sorted: items() does not sort names
  ...
Round 2: 9 passed, 0 failed, 0 errors, coverage 96%

TestPilot report for inventory.py
Likely bugs in your code:
  - test_items_sorted: items() does not sort names
```

## Project layout

```
testpilot/llm.py      Token Factory client, model routing, token usage tracking
testpilot/agent.py    the draft → run → triage → repair loop
testpilot/runner.py   isolated pytest + coverage runner
testpilot/cli.py      command line interface
app.py                Streamlit demo
examples/             sample module with planted bugs
tests/                offline tests for the agent loop (scripted fake LLM, no key needed)
```

Run TestPilot's own tests: `pytest`

## Safety note

Generated tests execute the module under test on your machine (in a temp directory, with a timeout). Only point TestPilot at code you trust.

